<onlyinclude>'''High-bandwidth memory''' (HBM) is a type of [[Dynamic random-access memory|DRAM]] made from memory chips stacked vertically and connected to a processor through a very wide interface. It moves large amounts of data per second, helping graphics processors, scientific simulations and AI accelerators.

== How it works ==
Tiny vertical electrical connections join the stacked chips. Advanced packaging puts the memory close to the processor, allowing many data wires to work in parallel. HBM stores data temporarily and needs regular refresh, like other DRAM.

== Why it matters ==
A program that moves large data sets can benefit from the extra ''bandwidth'' (data transferred per second). HBM does not necessarily respond to an individual request faster, and software that rarely waits for memory may see little improvement.

== Limits ==
HBM needs complex packaging and thermal management. Its capacity, cost, available bandwidth and actual performance depend on the particular product and workload; a manufacturer's peak bandwidth number is not a promise of application speed.


